WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.560
Does a cell simply measure how much Sonic hedgehog it sees?

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.720
Or does time matter too?

3
00:00:10.503 --> 00:00:12.863
Concentration turned out to be only half of the reading.

4
00:00:13.823 --> 00:00:15.463
Part of the other half lies in the brake.

5
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:21.667
Recall the brake. Patched holds the pathway off, and Sonic hedgehog lifts it.

6
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:23.747
Here is the twist.

7
00:00:24.787 --> 00:00:28.047
Patched1 is also one of the genes Sonic hedgehog turns on.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.200
That sounds backwards.

9
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.220
More receptor should mean more sensitivity.

10
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.220
But Patched is a brake.

11
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.820
In chick neural cells, signaling builds part of its own brake.

12
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.420
Then it takes more signal to keep going.

13
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.360
Picture two cells starting at once.

14
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.240
One gets a low dose of Sonic hedgehog.

15
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.340
The other gets a high dose.

16
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.160
Both make more Patched as they go.

17
00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:57.583
Predict. In which cell does signaling fade first?

18
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:04.727
The low-dose cell.

19
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:06.707
Its signaling soon fades.

20
00:01:07.627 --> 00:01:09.567
The high-dose cell keeps signaling for longer.

21
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:16.337
In 2007, Eric Dessaud, James Briscoe and colleagues reported this in chick neural

22
00:01:16.337 --> 00:01:17.337
cells.

23
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.260
The cells adapt. Signaling inside them fades, partly through rising Patched1.

24
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.350
Higher concentrations kept signaling going longer.

25
00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:29.830
A level becomes a length of time.

26
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.340
Now test it. Hold the dose fixed, and change only how long the cells are exposed.

27
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.620
What should longer exposure do?

28
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.750
In 2010, the same laboratory did this in chick explants.

29
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:54.233
At a fixed dose, longer exposure gave progressively more ventral identities.

30
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:58.293
The time a cell spends signaling is part of its address.

31
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:03.733
Roughly, a cell's identity tracks how much signaling it has had so far.

32
00:02:04.683 --> 00:02:08.763
It also needs the signal to continue for a while to keep that identity.

33
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:15.187
In chick explants, cutting the signal off with cyclopamine made cells fall back to

34
00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:16.187
more dorsal fates.

35
00:02:17.117 --> 00:02:20.577
Duration carries information in many signaling pathways.

36
00:02:21.577 --> 00:02:25.237
Here, it helps set which positional identity a progenitor adopts.

37
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.880
The simulation in this lesson lets you set both the level and the time, and watch

38
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:31.880
the domain change.

39
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.610
The takeaway: a progenitor reads both how much signal it gets, and for how long.
